1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
Block two.

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:05,050
Why training fails, and when a hidden layer pays off.

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:12,600
On the page, this block is the sections "Why training fails" and "When a hidden layer pays off: epistasis in a protein".

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,250
Everything in Block one worked.

5
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:19,100
In this block we break it, deliberately, one thing at a time.

6
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:24,825
Three distinct failures, each with a cause you can name and a fix that follows from the cause.

7
00:00:24,825 --> 00:00:34,825
And then the opposite case from the breast-cancer data: a problem where the hidden layer is worth a great deal, and where you can watch how much data it takes before that pays off.

8
00:00:34,825 --> 00:00:42,175
The method throughout, which is also how you should debug your own models: change one thing, see what happens, explain why.

9
00:00:42,775 --> 00:00:47,450
The dataset for this block is lovely, and worth understanding properly.

10
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:52,475
GB1 is a small domain of protein G, which binds immunoglobulin G.

11
00:00:52,475 --> 00:01:03,975
Wu and colleagues took four positions in it, V thirty-nine, D forty, G forty-one and V fifty-four, and made every possible combination of amino acids at those four positions.

12
00:01:03,975 --> 00:01:08,875
Twenty to the fourth power, which is a hundred and sixty thousand variants.

13
00:01:08,875 --> 00:01:12,900
Then they measured how well each one binds, by deep sequencing.

14
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:17,250
So this is something rare and precious: a complete landscape.

15
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:23,825
Not a sample, not a selection of interesting mutants, but every combination of four positions, measured.

16
00:01:23,825 --> 00:01:29,500
Our task is regression: predict the logarithm of fitness from the four amino acids.

17
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:36,325
We use twenty thousand variants for training, five thousand for validation and twenty thousand for testing.

18
00:01:36,325 --> 00:01:39,550
Two numbers to anchor everything in this block.

19
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:46,975
Predicting the mean for everything gives a mean absolute error of nought point four eight eight: that is the do-nothing baseline.

20
00:01:46,975 --> 00:01:53,375
A working network, sixty-four hidden units in each of two layers, reaches nought point two five zero.

21
00:01:53,375 --> 00:01:59,325
Every failure in this block starts from that working network and changes exactly one thing.

22
00:01:59,925 --> 00:02:05,475
Failure one, and it is the most instructive because the cause is so simple once you see it.

23
00:02:05,475 --> 00:02:12,550
Instead of initialising the weights to small random numbers, initialise them all to the same value, nought point one.

24
00:02:12,550 --> 00:02:17,200
Result: validation mean absolute error nought point three five two.

25
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:25,025
Barely better than a purely additive model, which gets nought point three seven one, and far from our network's nought point two five.

26
00:02:25,025 --> 00:02:25,850
Why?

27
00:02:25,850 --> 00:02:29,125
Think about two hidden units in the first layer.

28
00:02:29,125 --> 00:02:35,000
They have identical weights, so they see the same input and compute exactly the same output.

29
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,800
Then, when the gradient comes back, they are in identical positions in the network, so they receive exactly the same gradient.

30
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:45,325
So they update identically.

31
00:02:45,325 --> 00:02:47,675
So they are still identical.

32
00:02:47,675 --> 00:02:50,150
And that never, ever breaks.

33
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:56,150
Your sixty-four hidden units are sixty-four copies of one unit, for the entire training run.

34
00:02:56,150 --> 00:03:01,800
You have a network with one hidden unit and sixty-four times the computational cost.

35
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:09,025
The fix is random initialisation, and that is all it is for: breaking the symmetry so that units can specialise.

36
00:03:09,025 --> 00:03:15,550
PyTorch does it by default, so you will probably never hit this unless you deliberately set weights yourself.

37
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:18,725
But I teach it because of what it illustrates.

38
00:03:18,725 --> 00:03:22,375
The gradient was computed correctly at every step.

39
00:03:22,375 --> 00:03:25,450
Backpropagation did its job perfectly.

40
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:30,025
And the training was useless, because of a property of the starting point.

41
00:03:30,025 --> 00:03:32,925
Correct gradients are not sufficient.

42
00:03:33,525 --> 00:03:39,525
Failure two is the famous one, and it governed what architectures were possible for twenty years.

43
00:03:39,525 --> 00:03:46,575
Remember how backpropagation works: going backwards, you multiply by one activation derivative per layer.

44
00:03:46,575 --> 00:03:50,200
Each layer's derivative is a factor in a product.

45
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:58,100
Now, the sigmoid's derivative is at most nought point two five, and that maximum occurs only at exactly z equals zero.

46
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:05,300
Away from the centre it is far smaller: at z equals six, the derivative is nought point zero zero two five.

47
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:09,925
So each sigmoid layer multiplies the gradient by at most a quarter.

48
00:04:09,925 --> 00:04:17,425
Six layers multiply it by at most a quarter to the sixth power, which is about nought point zero zero zero two four.

49
00:04:17,425 --> 00:04:19,850
And that is the best case.

50
00:04:19,850 --> 00:04:29,875
Here is the measurement, on a freshly initialised six-layer network: the gradient reaching the first layer is ten million times smaller than the gradient at the last layer.

51
00:04:29,875 --> 00:04:35,075
The exact figure is ten million, twenty-two thousand, eight hundred and fifty-two.

52
00:04:35,075 --> 00:04:39,075
With tanh, the same ratio is three hundred and forty-three.

53
00:04:39,075 --> 00:04:42,700
With ReLU, one thousand nine hundred and twenty-six.

54
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:46,525
Think about what a factor of ten million means in practice.

55
00:04:46,525 --> 00:04:50,025
The last layer is learning at a reasonable pace.

56
00:04:50,025 --> 00:04:56,600
The first layer is receiving a signal so small that, in the number of steps you can afford, it does not move.

57
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:03,175
The early layers of your deep network are, for practical purposes, frozen at their random initial values.

58
00:05:03,175 --> 00:05:08,050
And the early layers are the ones that build the features everything else depends on.

59
00:05:08,050 --> 00:05:11,025
That is the vanishing gradient problem.

60
00:05:11,625 --> 00:05:22,225
Let us look at the three activations side by side, purely in terms of what fraction of the gradient each one passes backwards, because that is the quantity that matters here.

61
00:05:22,225 --> 00:05:28,925
The sigmoid: derivative at most nought point two five, at the centre, and shrinking fast in both directions.

62
00:05:28,925 --> 00:05:33,325
At z equals six it is nought point zero zero two five.

63
00:05:33,325 --> 00:05:37,775
So the sigmoid shrinks the gradient always, even at its best.

64
00:05:37,775 --> 00:05:49,500
Tanh: derivative is one at zero, which means it passes the gradient through unchanged there, and it also vanishes in the tails: at z equals six, nought point zero zero zero zero two.

65
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:54,875
So tanh is much better than sigmoid near the centre and just as bad far from it.

66
00:05:54,875 --> 00:06:01,350
ReLU: the derivative is exactly one for positive inputs and exactly zero for negative ones.

67
00:06:01,350 --> 00:06:03,350
Nothing in between.

68
00:06:03,350 --> 00:06:10,925
For any unit that is active, the gradient passes backwards completely unchanged, however many layers it travels through.

69
00:06:10,925 --> 00:06:19,900
That is why ReLU is the default for hidden layers in essentially everything modern, and it is a very simple reason: it does not shrink the gradient.

70
00:06:19,900 --> 00:06:22,475
The cost is on its other side.

71
00:06:22,475 --> 00:06:33,125
A unit whose input is negative passes back exactly zero, so it gets no gradient and does not update, and if it is negative for every example it is permanently dead.

72
00:06:33,125 --> 00:06:39,550
That is the subject of the first discussion problem, and it is why variants like leaky ReLU exist.

73
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:44,725
Here is the whole thing measured on GB1, which makes it concrete.

74
00:06:44,725 --> 00:06:53,300
Six layers with sigmoid activations, trained with plain stochastic gradient descent: the error stays at about nought point four eight.

75
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:56,050
That is the mean-predictor baseline.

76
00:06:56,050 --> 00:07:00,200
After all that training, the network has learned nothing at all.

77
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:04,225
The same architecture with tanh: nought point three two one.

78
00:07:04,225 --> 00:07:07,100
With ReLU: nought point three one two.

79
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:12,525
So the fix for a vanishing gradient is to change what the gradient has to pass through.

80
00:07:12,525 --> 00:07:15,025
ReLU instead of sigmoid.

81
00:07:15,025 --> 00:07:22,025
Initialisation scaled to the size of each layer, so that signals neither shrink nor explode as they propagate.

82
00:07:22,025 --> 00:07:26,225
Normalisation layers, which re-centre the activations.

83
00:07:26,225 --> 00:07:33,725
And residual connections, which give the gradient a path that skips layers entirely, which is Session ten's ResNet.

84
00:07:33,725 --> 00:07:37,925
Now, one more measurement, which is the subtle part of this slide.

85
00:07:37,925 --> 00:07:43,325
Train the same sigmoid network with Adam, and it reaches nought point two nine one.

86
00:07:43,325 --> 00:07:44,925
Much better.

87
00:07:44,925 --> 00:07:47,950
Has Adam fixed the vanishing gradient?

88
00:07:47,950 --> 00:07:48,850
No.

89
00:07:48,850 --> 00:07:51,925
The gradients are just as small as they were.

90
00:07:51,925 --> 00:07:59,325
What Adam does, as the next slides explain, is scale up the step size for weights whose gradients are consistently tiny.

91
00:07:59,325 --> 00:08:02,000
So it compensates for the symptom.

92
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:08,775
The signal reaching the first layer is still ten million times weaker and carries just as little information.

93
00:08:08,775 --> 00:08:15,800
That distinction matters when you debug: a workaround that improves the number is not the same as a fix for the cause.

94
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:19,125
The architectural fixes are the real ones.

95
00:08:19,125 --> 00:08:21,900
And this problem is not historical.

96
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:32,050
It comes back in deep convolutional networks in Session ten, which is why residual connections exist, and in recurrent networks and Transformers in Session eleven.

97
00:08:32,650 --> 00:08:36,975
Failure three: the step size, and which optimiser takes the step.

98
00:08:36,975 --> 00:08:41,575
Four numbers from GB1, all with the same network and data.

99
00:08:41,575 --> 00:08:48,450
Adam with a learning rate of nought point one: the error jumps around and never gets below nought point two nine eight.

100
00:08:48,450 --> 00:08:50,300
Too big a step.

101
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:58,425
Plain stochastic gradient descent with a learning rate of nought point zero one: nought point three zero five in twenty epochs.

102
00:08:58,425 --> 00:09:00,975
Not diverging, just crawling.

103
00:09:00,975 --> 00:09:06,375
Adam with a learning rate of nought point zero zero one: nought point two five zero.

104
00:09:06,375 --> 00:09:08,875
That is our working network.

105
00:09:08,875 --> 00:09:15,950
The practical advice is boring and reliable: start with Adam and a learning rate of about ten to the minus three.

106
00:09:15,950 --> 00:09:22,475
That is the default that works most often across most problems, and when you have something working you can tune.

107
00:09:22,475 --> 00:09:27,675
And then the habit that matters more than the choice: always watch the validation curve.

108
00:09:27,675 --> 00:09:30,175
Not the final number, the curve.

109
00:09:30,175 --> 00:09:34,475
A curve that bounces wildly means the learning rate is too high.

110
00:09:34,475 --> 00:09:39,850
A curve that descends in a straight line and has not flattened means you stopped too early.

111
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:46,675
A curve that is flat from the beginning means something in Block two is wrong, and the previous two slides are where to look.

112
00:09:47,275 --> 00:09:51,625
Why should an optimiser be able to beat plain gradient descent at all?

113
00:09:51,625 --> 00:09:56,750
The gradient is the direction of steepest descent, so what is there to improve?

114
00:09:56,750 --> 00:10:00,300
Here is the picture, and it is the key intuition.

115
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:06,025
Imagine a long narrow valley, a hundred times steeper across than it is along its floor.

116
00:10:06,025 --> 00:10:10,750
Question first: in a valley like that, which way does the gradient point?

117
00:10:10,750 --> 00:10:12,750
Mostly across.

118
00:10:12,750 --> 00:10:17,050
The steepest direction is up the side wall, not along the floor.

119
00:10:17,050 --> 00:10:21,675
But the direction you need to travel, to get to the bottom, is along the floor.

120
00:10:21,675 --> 00:10:29,825
So plain gradient descent zigzags violently from one wall to the other, making very little progress in the direction that matters.

121
00:10:29,825 --> 00:10:35,025
On the slide, after sixty steps it has got the loss down to nought point zero nine.

122
00:10:35,025 --> 00:10:37,875
Momentum fixes this elegantly.

123
00:10:37,875 --> 00:10:44,725
Instead of stepping in the current gradient's direction, you keep a running average of recent gradients and step in that.

124
00:10:44,725 --> 00:10:47,925
Picture a heavy ball rolling down the valley.

125
00:10:47,925 --> 00:10:53,600
The across-valley components point one way and then the other and cancel out in the average.

126
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:57,725
The along-valley components all point the same way and add up.

127
00:10:57,725 --> 00:11:02,700
After sixty steps, momentum has reached nought point zero zero zero four.

128
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:11,850
Adam adds a second idea on top: a separate step size for every individual weight, scaled by how large that weight's gradients have recently been.

129
00:11:11,850 --> 00:11:20,675
Weights with consistently small gradients get larger steps, which is exactly the behaviour that partially rescued the sigmoid network two slides ago.

130
00:11:20,675 --> 00:11:24,200
Adam reaches nought point zero zero six here.

131
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:30,050
Note that Adam is not the winner in this particular picture: well-tuned momentum ends lower.

132
00:11:30,050 --> 00:11:34,175
Adam is a very good default, not a universal champion.

133
00:11:34,175 --> 00:11:39,150
And please keep the two problems apart, because they get conflated constantly.

134
00:11:39,150 --> 00:11:44,875
Vanishing gradients are about the signal being destroyed, and the fixes are architectural.

135
00:11:44,875 --> 00:11:50,050
The valley is about the shape of the landscape, and the fixes are in the optimiser.

136
00:11:50,650 --> 00:11:58,250
Your lab reproduces these failures on the blood-cell images, so that you have seen them with your own hands rather than on my slides.

137
00:11:58,250 --> 00:12:07,850
With four or eight sigmoid layers, the network predicts a single class for everything, scoring nought point one nine eight, which is the share of the most common class.

138
00:12:07,850 --> 00:12:12,725
That is the vanishing gradient, producing the most useless possible model.

139
00:12:12,725 --> 00:12:21,475
And the lab measures it rather than asserting it: the gradient reaching the first layer is two million times smaller with eight hidden layers than with one.

140
00:12:21,475 --> 00:12:33,875
On optimisers, with a hundred-and-twenty-eight-unit network: plain SGD reaches nought point seven six one, SGD with momentum nought point eight zero three, and Adam nought point eight zero two.

141
00:12:33,875 --> 00:12:41,625
Notice that momentum and Adam are essentially tied here, which is a healthy corrective to the idea that Adam is always best.

142
00:12:41,625 --> 00:12:47,275
The rule for the lab, and for your own work: change one thing, see what happens, explain why.

143
00:12:47,275 --> 00:12:50,600
If you cannot explain why, you have not finished.

144
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:57,700
Let me put the three failures on one slide, because this is the diagnostic list I would like you to carry.

145
00:12:57,700 --> 00:13:00,525
One: every weight starts the same.

146
00:13:00,525 --> 00:13:05,025
The symptom is that a wide layer performs like a layer with one unit.

147
00:13:05,025 --> 00:13:11,175
The cause is that identical units receive identical gradients and stay identical for ever.

148
00:13:11,175 --> 00:13:14,250
The fix is random initialisation.

149
00:13:14,250 --> 00:13:18,075
Two: a deep network with saturating activations.

150
00:13:18,075 --> 00:13:25,550
The symptom is that the loss barely moves, especially early in training, and the early layers stay near their initial values.

151
00:13:25,550 --> 00:13:30,800
The cause is that each layer multiplies the gradient by at most nought point two five.

152
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:36,725
The fixes are ReLU, scaled initialisation, normalisation and residual connections.

153
00:13:36,725 --> 00:13:38,925
Three: the learning rate.

154
00:13:38,925 --> 00:13:46,525
The symptom is either a loss that jumps around or never settles, or one that descends so slowly you run out of patience.

155
00:13:46,525 --> 00:13:54,275
The cause is one step size applied to every weight, in a landscape where the right step size differs wildly between directions.

156
00:13:54,275 --> 00:13:59,800
The fixes are Adam at about ten to the minus three, and watching the validation curve.

157
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:02,700
Notice what these three have in common.

158
00:14:02,700 --> 00:14:06,125
In all of them the gradient was computed correctly.

159
00:14:06,125 --> 00:14:08,700
Backpropagation never failed.

160
00:14:08,700 --> 00:14:13,975
The failure was elsewhere: in the starting point, in the architecture, in the step size.

161
00:14:13,975 --> 00:14:18,975
Which is why understanding the loop is not the same as being able to train things.

162
00:14:19,575 --> 00:14:24,500
Now the other half of this block: when is a hidden layer actually worth having?

163
00:14:24,500 --> 00:14:27,100
Two models on GB1.

164
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:36,950
A linear model on the one-hot encoded sequence, which means every amino acid at every one of the four positions gets its own weight, and the four weights are added up.

165
00:14:36,950 --> 00:14:40,275
And our sixty-four by sixty-four network.

166
00:14:40,275 --> 00:14:43,625
Linear: R squared of nought point five zero.

167
00:14:43,625 --> 00:14:46,400
Network: nought point seven nine.

168
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:53,900
That is a large gap, and it is the opposite of the breast-cancer result, so the question is what is different about this data.

169
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:56,925
First, notice what the linear model is.

170
00:14:56,925 --> 00:14:59,325
Per-position weights, summed.

171
00:14:59,325 --> 00:15:04,125
That is a position weight matrix from Session five, in different clothing.

172
00:15:04,125 --> 00:15:10,175
Which means it carries the same assumption: each position contributes independently of the others.

173
00:15:10,175 --> 00:15:14,925
And in a protein that is false, in a way that has a name: epistasis.

174
00:15:14,925 --> 00:15:20,825
The effect of a mutation at position thirty-nine depends on what is at position fifty-four.

175
00:15:20,825 --> 00:15:29,475
A substitution that is tolerated in one background is fatal in another, because the residues touch each other, or share a packing environment.

176
00:15:29,475 --> 00:15:34,000
Epistasis is Session seven's XOR, in a real protein.

177
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:43,675
And a sum of per-position terms cannot represent it, however carefully you fit the weights, for exactly the reason a single weighted sum cannot do XOR.

178
00:15:43,675 --> 00:15:46,925
Two further numbers that make the point sharper.

179
00:15:46,925 --> 00:15:55,550
Weight decay and dropout, which are Block three's tools, improve the network a little: nought point seven nine five and nought point seven nine eight.

180
00:15:55,550 --> 00:16:07,650
And making the network four times wider, two hundred and fifty-six units per layer and eighty-six thousand, seven hundred and eighty-five weights, gives nought point seven seven nine, which is slightly worse.

181
00:16:07,650 --> 00:16:12,200
So the gain came from having a hidden layer at all, not from size.

182
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:17,525
More capacity, past the point where you can represent the interactions, buys nothing.

183
00:16:18,125 --> 00:16:23,400
Look at how the two models fail, because the shape of the failure says what is missing.

184
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:28,925
Both panels plot predicted against measured fitness for twenty thousand test variants.

185
00:16:28,925 --> 00:16:32,650
A perfect model would put every point on the diagonal.

186
00:16:32,650 --> 00:16:39,125
The additive model, on the left, R squared nought point five zero: the cloud is squashed towards the middle.

187
00:16:39,125 --> 00:16:43,200
It predicts almost nothing as very fit or very unfit.

188
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:50,575
Ask yourself before I answer: what can a sum of per-position weights not represent, and what would that do to the extremes?

189
00:16:50,575 --> 00:16:56,400
The very fit variants are the ones where a particular combination works especially well together.

190
00:16:56,400 --> 00:17:03,850
That is precisely the interaction the additive model cannot express, so it averages over it and predicts something middling.

191
00:17:03,850 --> 00:17:06,325
The same at the unfit end.

192
00:17:06,325 --> 00:17:11,900
The additive model compresses the range because the extremes are made of interactions.

193
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:20,175
The network, in the middle panel, R squared nought point seven nine, follows the diagonal across the whole range, including the extremes.

194
00:17:20,175 --> 00:17:27,500
And the third panel is the one to remember for your own projects: test R squared against the size of the training set.

195
00:17:27,500 --> 00:17:29,600
We go to it next.

196
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:36,050
Here is that third panel in numbers, and it is the most practically useful slide in the block.

197
00:17:36,050 --> 00:17:42,525
With five hundred training variants: linear nought point four one three, network nought point four two nine.

198
00:17:42,525 --> 00:17:43,600
A tie.

199
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:49,525
With five thousand: linear nought point four nine two, network nought point seven one six.

200
00:17:49,525 --> 00:17:55,425
With sixty thousand: linear nought point four eight five, network nought point eight one seven.

201
00:17:55,425 --> 00:18:00,350
Read the two columns separately, because they behave completely differently.

202
00:18:00,350 --> 00:18:02,675
The linear model is flat.

203
00:18:02,675 --> 00:18:06,800
Nought point four one, nought point four nine, nought point four nine.

204
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:12,500
More data does not help it, because it ran out of model long before it ran out of data.

205
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:18,475
It cannot represent the interactions, so additional examples of interactions teach it nothing.

206
00:18:18,475 --> 00:18:21,750
The network climbs steadily and pulls away.

207
00:18:21,750 --> 00:18:26,875
So the hidden layer's advantage is not free: it has to be bought with data.

208
00:18:26,875 --> 00:18:34,750
At five hundred variants the flexible model has no advantage at all, because it cannot yet tell a real interaction from noise.

209
00:18:34,750 --> 00:18:37,175
By five thousand it can.

210
00:18:37,175 --> 00:18:47,925
That is the general shape of the trade, and it explains the breast-cancer result too: there the data was additive, so no amount of data would have given the hidden layer anything to learn.

211
00:18:47,925 --> 00:18:54,825
And it connects backwards: this is the limitation of profiles that Session five noted, in a modern setting.

212
00:18:54,825 --> 00:19:06,000
Session twelve comes back to it with pairwise models learned from multiple alignments, which is a way of capturing interactions without needing a hundred and sixty thousand measured variants.

